export const slides = [
  { tag:'DCS SEMINAR · MODULE 07', title:'A little structure.\nA lot less noise.', subtitle:'Low-Density Parity-Check Codes and Their Application in Modern Communication Systems', formula:'LDPC · sparse graphs · iterative decoding · channel capacity', note:'A visual journey from Hamming codes to modern error correction.', speaker:'Open the live lab before presenting. Ask: if a radio receives the wrong bit, how could it know? The answer is structured redundancy. This seminar connects Module 7 linear block codes, error correction, and channel capacity.' },
  { tag:'01 / THE PROBLEM', title:'Noise changes the message.', subtitle:'A receiver sees a measurement, not a perfect bit. Error correction gives it evidence beyond that measurement.', columns:[['Noise','Thermal noise and interference shift received amplitudes. A hard decision can flip a bit.'],['Redundancy','Transmit extra, structured bits. Valid messages obey shared constraints.'],['Recovery','Use those constraints to infer the most likely transmitted codeword.']], formula:'information bits → encoder → noisy channel → decoder', speaker:'Redundancy costs bandwidth or time. We exchange that cost for reliability at a lower signal level. Distinguish error detection from correction: detecting a violated rule is easier than finding the original message.' },
  { tag:'02 / THE SYLLABUS CONNECTION', title:'Start with Hamming.', subtitle:'Hamming (7,4) carries 4 information bits in 7 transmitted bits. Its minimum distance is 3.', columns:[['Shared foundation','Both Hamming and LDPC are linear block codes defined by parity constraints.'],['Hamming (7,4)','A syndrome identifies any one-bit error. Two-bit errors are not safely corrected by the basic decoder.'],['LDPC','Longer blocks, sparse graphs, soft information, and iterative local updates.']], formula:'s = H rᵀ    over GF(2)', note:'Hamming (7,4) is not extended Hamming (8,4), which adds SECDED behavior.', speaker:'The syndrome is zero for a valid codeword. With the Hamming parity-check matrix, every column is a different nonzero binary vector. One error produces the column associated with its position. Do not claim that basic Hamming (7,4) can reliably distinguish every double error while correcting singles.' },
  { tag:'03 / THE MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATION', title:'A code is a set of rules.', subtitle:'H has m rows and n columns. Every row is one parity equation. Arithmetic is modulo 2.', columns:[['Codeword','A length-n binary vector x satisfying every parity equation.'],['Dimension','k = n − rank(H), using rank over GF(2). Dependent rows do not add new rules.'],['Code rate','R = k/n. A lower rate spends more transmitted bits on redundancy.']], formula:'H x = 0 (mod 2)       R = [n − rank(H)] / n', speaker:'Write a row such as x1 XOR x3 XOR x6 = 0. If five values in a six-bit check are known, the sixth is implied. The equation uses a column-vector convention; the Hamming slide uses the equivalent received row-vector convention with a transpose.' },
  { tag:'04 / WHY LOW DENSITY?', title:'Sparse is the whole point.', subtitle:'Most entries of H are zero. Each bit participates in only a few checks, even as the block becomes large.', columns:[['Variable degree','This lab uses three edges per variable node.'],['Check degree','Each parity check has six variable neighbors.'],['Work per iteration','Linear in the number of graph edges for a fixed-degree construction.']], formula:'E = n dᵥ = m d꜀       here: dᵥ = 3, d꜀ = 6', note:'The teaching graph may have short cycles. Standards use carefully designed constructions.', speaker:'A dense matrix would connect nearly everything and make iterative processing expensive. Sparse graphs keep each update local. Four-cycles correlate messages quickly; avoiding short cycles and selecting degree distributions matters for performance. The nominal design rate is one half; actual rank determines the true rate.' },
  { tag:'05 / ENCODING', title:'Build a valid codeword.', subtitle:'Choose information bits. Solve the parity constraints. Transmit all n bits.', columns:[['Choose','Pick k free coordinates after Gaussian elimination over GF(2).'],['Solve','Calculate pivot coordinates from the reduced parity equations.'],['Verify','Check that Hx = 0 before transmission.']], formula:'u ∈ {0,1}ᵏ   →   x ∈ {0,1}ⁿ   with Hx = 0', note:'The lab encodes using GF(2) elimination. Production QC-LDPC encoders exploit matrix structure.', speaker:'Sparse H does not imply a sparse generator matrix. Generic encoding can be more expensive than decoding. Structured quasi-cyclic constructions allow efficient encoding in real systems. The lab sends randomized valid codewords in the interactive sample.' },
  { tag:'06 / THE CHANNEL MODEL', title:'Bits become a waveform.', subtitle:'Binary phase-shift keying sends 0 as +1 and 1 as −1. Add white Gaussian noise.', columns:[['Modulate','s = 1 − 2x, giving a unit-energy coded symbol.'],['Corrupt','y = s + z, where z is Gaussian with variance σ².'],['Normalize','The SNR slider is Eb/N₀ per information bit, not per coded symbol.']], formula:'σ² = 1 / [2R · 10^(Eb/N₀[dB]/10)]', note:'Real-valued BPSK, one coded bit per real channel use, symbol energy normalized to one.', speaker:'Rate appears in the noise normalization because one information bit consumes 1/R coded symbols. Without that factor, comparing code rates or reporting Eb/N0 would be misleading. This is a memoryless AWGN model; it does not model fading, intersymbol interference, or synchronization.' },
  { tag:'07 / SOFT INFORMATION', title:'“Probably zero” is useful.', subtitle:'A hard decision discards confidence. The log-likelihood ratio keeps both the sign and strength of the evidence.', columns:[['Positive LLR','Favors bit 0. Larger magnitude means stronger confidence.'],['Negative LLR','Favors bit 1. The decoder can revise its initial decision.'],['Near zero','The channel offers little evidence. Neighboring checks matter more.']], formula:'Lch = ln[P(y | x=0) / P(y | x=1)] = 2y / σ²', speaker:'Equal prior probabilities are assumed. Show two received values, y=0.01 and y=2. Both hard-decide to zero, yet their confidence is different. A soft decoder uses that distinction. The lab clips messages to magnitude 24 for numerical stability.' },
  { tag:'08 / THE TANNER GRAPH', title:'Turn a matrix into a conversation.', subtitle:'Circles represent bits. Squares represent checks. An edge exists exactly when Hⱼᵢ = 1.', columns:[['Variable nodes','Keep channel evidence and incoming check messages.'],['Check nodes','Enforce an even XOR sum across their neighbors.'],['Extrinsic evidence','A message excludes the edge it is sent back on. Avoid immediate self-reinforcement.']], formula:'one matrix entry of 1 = one graph edge', speaker:'Rotate the graph and click a node in the lab. Trace its highlighted edges. The 3D layout is only a visualization: rotating it does not change the matrix or code. A Tanner graph is bipartite; edges connect variables to checks, never two variables directly.' },
  { tag:'09 / BELIEF PROPAGATION', title:'Local messages. Global inference.', subtitle:'The sum-product decoder alternates check updates and variable updates in the log domain.', formula:'L꜀→ᵥ = 2 atanh[ ∏ tanh(Lᵥ′→꜀ / 2) ]', columns:[['Check update','Combine incoming beliefs from every other variable connected to this check.'],['Variable update','Add channel LLR to check messages from every other check.'],['Hard decision','Sum all incoming check messages and channel evidence; decide from the sign.']], note:'Normalized min-sum approximates the check update using sign products and minimum magnitude; this lab uses α = 0.8.', speaker:'Products exclude the destination variable. Variable-to-check sums exclude the destination check. The posterior sum includes every check. This flooding schedule updates all checks before updating variables. On a tree this computes exact marginals; on a graph with cycles it is an iterative approximation.' },
  { tag:'10 / LIVE DEMO', title:'Give the receiver a harder day.', subtitle:'Open the lab. Move Eb/N₀, generate new noise, and watch confidence evolve one iteration at a time.', columns:[['1 · Start at 2 dB','Inspect the red bit errors and unsatisfied checks.'],['2 · Press play','Follow the LLR bars and parity-check count. Pause or step at any iteration.'],['3 · Lower the SNR','Notice failed convergence and occasional wrong valid codewords.']], formula:'Noise is random. Recovery is not guaranteed.', action:'Open the interactive lab', speaker:'Allow about two minutes. Use the default reproducible sample first. New noise changes the random seed; changing SNR keeps the same noise draw scaled to a different level. Move to Performance to show the difference between one frame and Monte Carlo statistics.' },
  { tag:'11 / MEASURING PERFORMANCE', title:'One recovered frame is not a curve.', subtitle:'Bit error rate counts incorrect bits across many independent frames at each Eb/N₀.', columns:[['BER','Number of incorrect coded bits divided by transmitted coded bits.'],['Waterfall','Many long, well-designed LDPC codes show a steep BER improvement above a threshold.'],['Finite samples','Zero observed errors does not prove zero BER. Collect more frames.']], formula:'BER = error bits / tested bits', note:'The lab measures 160 frames at each of 17 SNR points. Down arrows mark zero errors at 1/N as a plotting indicator, not a confidence bound.', speaker:'The performance chart compares the received hard decisions and decoded codeword bits on the same coded BPSK channel. It is not a fair uncoded-versus-coded information-throughput benchmark. Short teaching codes can show rough curves and poor gains. A Hamming comparison would need matching energy normalization and enough trials.' },
  { tag:'12 / THE SHANNON CONNECTION', title:'A limit, not a decoder.', subtitle:'Below channel capacity, suitable sequences of sufficiently long codes can make error probability arbitrarily small.', columns:[['General AWGN','For a real Gaussian input: C = ½ log₂(1 + S/N) bits per real channel use.'],['BPSK constraint','Binary input limits capacity to at most 1 bit per real use. The lab plots I(X;Y) for BPSK.'],['Finite block length','A small code with a finite iteration budget cannot promise capacity-achieving performance.']], formula:'Reliable rate requires R < C', note:'At R = 1/2, the BPSK-constrained asymptotic Eb/N₀ threshold is about 0.19 dB. The wideband Gaussian limit −1.59 dB is a different operating limit.', speaker:'Capacity is a property of a channel and its input constraints, not the performance of a particular decoder. The lab computes binary-input mutual information by Gaussian integration and solves C=R numerically. Do not place the universal −1.59 dB label at a rate-one-half BPSK threshold.' },
  { tag:'13 / REAL-WORLD COMMUNICATION', title:'These checks travel everywhere.', subtitle:'LDPC moved from Gallager’s 1962 idea to large, practical communication and storage systems.', columns:[['5G NR','LDPC protects data channels. Polar codes handle control information. See 3GPP TS 38.212.'],['Satellite & Wi-Fi','DVB-S2 combines LDPC with an outer BCH code. Modern Wi-Fi supports LDPC.'],['Storage','Soft reads and LDPC help recover data as flash memory becomes noisier.']], formula:'Structure in the code. Reliability in the system.', speaker:'Avoid saying all 5G channels use LDPC or that all Wi-Fi traffic uses it. Different standards and configurations select different codes. Practical decoders trade throughput, power, memory, iteration count, and reliability.' },
  { tag:'14 / TAKEAWAYS', title:'A little structure goes a long way.', subtitle:'Hamming gives the foundation. Sparse graphs make local inference practical. Long, carefully designed codes approach capacity.', columns:[['Remember the rule','Valid codewords satisfy Hx = 0 over GF(2).'],['Remember the process','Soft information, extrinsic messages, iterative updates.'],['Remember the caveat','Parity success means a valid codeword. It does not prove the original message was recovered.']], formula:'Questions worth asking: rate? block length? channel? decoder? evidence?', speaker:'Close by returning to the opening question. The receiver does not know the transmitted bits; it combines noisy evidence with shared constraints. In the lab we know the original codeword to visualize true errors, which a real receiver cannot directly observe.' },
  { tag:'15 / REFERENCES & REPRODUCIBILITY', title:'Follow the evidence.', subtitle:'Foundations, standards, and the exact assumptions behind this seminar.', columns:[['Foundations','R. G. Gallager (1962), Low-Density Parity-Check Codes, IEEE TIT. C. E. Shannon (1948), A Mathematical Theory of Communication.'],['Standards','3GPP TS 38.212, NR multiplexing and channel coding. ETSI EN 302 307-1, DVB-S2. IEEE 802.11, wireless LAN.'],['Lab settings','Regular (3,6), n = 24/48/96, actual GF(2) rank, BPSK/AWGN, flooding schedule, LLR clipping ±24, deterministic seeds.']], note:'DOI: 10.1109/TIT.1962.1057683 · 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1948.tb01338.x. The downloadable deck includes speaker notes and an editable design.', speaker:'The source code and README document all simulation assumptions. Use the CSV export for a reproducible single-frame report. The BER chart uses all-zero codewords: for a linear code on a symmetric BPSK/AWGN channel with a symmetric decoder, this does not restrict the ensemble BER. Cite the standards for applications, not the small lab for standard performance.' }
];
